# Probability {.unnumbered}



## Why do we need statistics? 


### Ash dieback

Ash dieback (*Hymenoscyphus fraxineus*) is a fungus which originated in Asia. It doesn’t cause much damage on its native hosts of the Manchurian ash (*Fraxinus mandshurica*) and the Chinese ash (*Fraxinus chinensis*) in its native range. However, its introduction to Europe about 30 years ago has devastated the European ash (*Fraxinus excelsior*) because our native ash species did not evolve with the fungus and this means it has no natural defence against it.

Cambridge City Council reports a steady decline in the health of the city's [ash tree](https://www.treeguideuk.co.uk/common-ash-tree-identification/) population as a result of the fungal outbreak. The evidence is a monitoring survey: a randomised sample of ninety-nine trees drawn in 2017 from the roughly 1,500 individual ash on the council's tree management database, resurveyed annually. By 2024, adding the higher defoliation bands, around 41% of the sampled trees (almost 41 trees) had lost more than a quarter of their foliage. The condition of the other 1,401 is unknown to us. Taken literally, the survey guarantees only that the true figure for the managed population lies somewhere between 41/1500 (2.7%) and 1442/1500 (96%). So on what basis does anyone report 41% of trees are affected?

The intuition is easy enough. If 99 trees are chosen at random and 41 are badly defoliated, it would be a remarkable coincidence if those were the only badly defoliated trees in the city. **We assume the sample resembles the population**. But how closely? Nobody would object to 43%, and everybody would probably object to 70%, yet it is hard to say by inspection whether 50% is plausible, or whether any reported annual changes belong only to our sample or is affecting all of our trees. Intuition runs out at exactly the point the ecology becomes interesting.

### Descriptive statistics

**Descriptive statistics** deal with the 99 trees actually surveyed. They summarise what is in front of you: the proportion in each defoliation band, the mean and standard deviation of crown loss, the change from one year to the next, a chart of the results. Descriptive statistics make no claim beyond the sampled trees, and within that limit they are not disputable. If 41 of your 99 trees had lost more than a quarter of their foliage, then 41 per cent is the sample proportion, and that is the end of the matter.

### Inferential statistics

**Inferential statistics** deal with the 1,500, and with the wider question the council actually cares about. They are the tools for *reasoning from the sample you have to the population you do not*, and for attaching a defensible statement of uncertainty to that reasoning. A confidence interval around the 41 per cent, a model relating crown condition to tree age or site type: these are attempts to say something about the trees nobody inspected based on the ones we did. Almost every question a biologist asks is of this kind. You did not measure every tree, every plot or every patient, and you are not interested in the ones you measured except as a route to the ones you did not.

### Why probability comes first. 

The theory of **inferential statistics** is built on top of **probability theory**. The following is basically background, there's not a lot of statistics here, its the fundamentals that we should understand in order to carry out statistics later. 


## What's the difference between probability and statistics?

Probability and statistics are related, but not identical, concepts. 

### Probability

**Probability** theory is the "doctrine of chances". It is a branch of mathematics that tells you how often different kinds of events will happen ^[And why probability might not be real (https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-024-04096-5)].  

Probability takes a **model of the world** and reasons forwards, from a known world to unknown data. You are told the rules, and you work out what you are likely to see.

In each case we start from a stated model of how the world works and asks what data that model tends to produce. The model is known; the data are not.

::: {.webex-check .webex-box}

Each question below gives you a model and asks about the data it produces. That
is the direction of reasoning we called probability.

Both parents are heterozygous (*Aa*) at a locus where *a* is fully recessive. What fraction of offspring are expected to show the recessive phenotype? <div class='webex-radiogroup' id='radio_RSOPGIFPGI'><label><input type="radio" autocomplete="off" name="radio_RSOPGIFPGI" value=""></input> <span>1/2</span></label><label><input type="radio" autocomplete="off" name="radio_RSOPGIFPGI" value="answer"></input> <span>1/4</span></label><label><input type="radio" autocomplete="off" name="radio_RSOPGIFPGI" value=""></input> <span>1/3</span></label><label><input type="radio" autocomplete="off" name="radio_RSOPGIFPGI" value=""></input> <span>3/4</span></label></div>


<div class='webex-solution'><button>I need a hint</button>


Write out the four equally likely combinations of one allele from each parent.
Only one of them produces an individual with no dominant allele at all.


</div>



<div class='webex-solution'><button>Show the answer</button>


**1/4.** Each parent passes on *A* or *a* with equal probability, so the four
equally likely offspring genotypes are *AA*, *Aa*, *aA* and *aa*. Only *aa*
lacks a dominant allele, so the recessive phenotype appears in one case out of
four. Note that this is a statement about expected proportions in the long run,
not a promise about any particular litter of four.


</div>


---

A clutch contains eight eggs. Assume each egg is independently equally likely to be male or female. What is the probability that all eight are female? <div class='webex-radiogroup' id='radio_KYNCJZBEWI'><label><input type="radio" autocomplete="off" name="radio_KYNCJZBEWI" value="answer"></input> <span>1/256</span></label><label><input type="radio" autocomplete="off" name="radio_KYNCJZBEWI" value=""></input> <span>1/64</span></label><label><input type="radio" autocomplete="off" name="radio_KYNCJZBEWI" value=""></input> <span>1/16</span></label><label><input type="radio" autocomplete="off" name="radio_KYNCJZBEWI" value=""></input> <span>1/8</span></label></div>


<div class='webex-solution'><button>I need a hint</button>


The first egg is female with probability 1/2. Given that, the second is female
with probability 1/2 as well, because the eggs are independent. Keep going.


</div>



<div class='webex-solution'><button>Show the answer</button>


**1/256, or about 0.4%.** Independence means the probabilities multiply, so the
answer is $(1/2)^8 = 1/256$. In R:


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
0.5^8
```
:::


Two things are worth noticing. First, an all-female clutch is unlikely but not
astonishing: in a study of 300 clutches you would expect to see one or two.
Second, the answer depends entirely on the two assumptions in the question. If
sex is not determined independently egg by egg, or the ratio is not 1:1, the
calculation no longer applies.


</div>


---

A pond snail occupies 30% of ponds in a region. You survey three ponds chosen at random and independently. What is the probability that you find the snail at none of them? <div class='webex-radiogroup' id='radio_YSSLIMXHVR'><label><input type="radio" autocomplete="off" name="radio_YSSLIMXHVR" value=""></input> <span>0.09</span></label><label><input type="radio" autocomplete="off" name="radio_YSSLIMXHVR" value=""></input> <span>0.027</span></label><label><input type="radio" autocomplete="off" name="radio_YSSLIMXHVR" value=""></input> <span>0.9</span></label><label><input type="radio" autocomplete="off" name="radio_YSSLIMXHVR" value="answer"></input> <span>0.343</span></label></div>


<div class='webex-solution'><button>I need a hint</button>


Work out the probability that a single pond is unoccupied first, then use the
same multiplication rule as the clutch question.


</div>



<div class='webex-solution'><button>Show the answer</button>


**0.343, or about 34%.** A given pond is unoccupied with probability
$1 - 0.3 = 0.7$, and the three ponds are independent, so the probability that
all three are unoccupied is $0.7^3 = 0.343$:


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
0.7^3
```
:::


So a survey of three ponds returns nothing at all about a third of the time,
even though the species is present in nearly a third of the region. Drawing a
blank is not evidence of absence when the sample is small. This is the same
idea as statistical power, which we meet formally in Session 3.


</div>


:::


### Statistics

**Statistics** reasons backwards, from known data to an unknown world. You hold the observations and you want the rule that produced them:

- Two heterozygous parents, and 40 of 100 offspring show the recessive phenotype. Is that consistent with simple Mendelian inheritance?

- A clutch of eight is all female. Chance, or does this species skew its sex ratio?

- Three ponds surveyed, no snails found. Is the snail absent from the region?

The same three situations, turned around. Now the data are the fixed thing you hold and the model is what you are chasing.

::: {.callout-note}
## Probability vs statistics

In probability the model is known and the data are uncertain.

In statistics the data are known and the model is uncertain.

Statistics is probability run backwards, and backwards is the harder direction, which is why the subject exists at all.

:::

## Defining probability {#sec-frequentist}

What is "probability"? While we mostly agree on the *rules* of probability, there is not a total consensus on what the word really *means*. In statistics there are two major approaches to probability, and the more dominant one defines probability as a **long-run frequency**.

Saying a fair coin has probability 0.5 of landing heads means that if you flip it over and over, the proportion of heads settles towards 0.5 as the number of flips grows. The probability is the value that proportion converges to. We would not be that surprised if 4/5 coin flips came up heads, we might be quite surprised if 9/10 coin flips came up heads, we would be **extremely suspicious** if 19/20 coin flips came up heads.

You can watch it happen, and you should, at the start of a sequence the **proportion** of heads can fluctuate wildly, but as the sample size grows we expect to see a *regression to the mean*

This is the **frequentist** definition of probability

