Do these three things in order. Each takes under a minute.

1. Press **Flip once** ten times, slowly. Watch the proportion after each flip. Say out loud whether it is heading anywhere.
2. Press **Flip 1000** and watch the line. Then read the milestone table underneath it: how far from 0.5 was the proportion after 10 flips, after 100, and after 1000?
3. Press **Start a new run** and flip 1000 again. Do it a third time. The finished runs stay on the chart as faint lines.

Then answer this, before reading on: the three runs took visibly different paths. Did they end up in visibly different places?


<div class='webex-solution'><button>Check your thinking</button>


They did not. Every run wanders somewhere different early on, because with ten flips a run of heads is unremarkable, and by a thousand flips they are all pressed up against 0.5 with nowhere much to go. What varies enormously at small numbers of observations varies hardly at all at large ones.

Note also what did *not* happen: no run landed exactly on 0.5 and stayed there. Convergence is not arrival.


</div>


::::
:::

Hold on to that picture. A quantity that is wild when you have few observations and steadies as you gather more is the seed of the whole workshop, and you will meet it again in the room, built out of measurements rather than coin flips.

This reading is objective, in that it is grounded in events that happen, and unambiguous, in that two people watching the same sequence must agree. It also has a real limit: it only speaks about things that can be repeated. Ask for the probability that one particular volcano erupts next decade and there is no long run to appeal to.

::: {.callout-note}
## There is a second reading, and it comes later

Statisticians agree almost completely about the rules probabilities obey and disagree about what the word means. A second school reads a probability as a *degree of belief*, which lets you attach one to a one-off event. We will meet that reading in the next session (known as the **Bayesian** view of probability), at the point where it makes a concrete difference to how you interpret a result. For now, note only that a choice has been made and that you were told about it.
:::

## Random variables

A **random variable** is a quantity whose value is settled by a random process. Before the process runs you cannot say what value it will take; after it runs you have one particular value, and running it again gives you a different value.

The mass of the next beetle you pull out of a pitfall trap is a random variable. So is the number of quadrats out of twenty that contain bramble. So is the outcome of a coin flip, coded 0 or 1.

Take a sample of five beetles and compute their mean mass. That mean is a number you calculated rather than a number you measured, so it feels solid. It is not. It was computed from five values that were themselves settled by a random process, so **the mean is a random variable too**. Draw five fresh beetles and you get a different mean.

### Why does this matter?

Someone repeating your study with a fresh five beetles gets a different number, and neither of you has the true population mean. The mean is random, but its randomness has structure. Draw five beetles a thousand times and the thousand means form a distribution with a centre and a width: they cluster around the population mean according to a **probability distribution** rather than scattering anywhere, and the width of the cluster depends on things you control, chiefly how many beetles you sampled. Because that structure is predictable, a single mean can be reported alongside a defensible statement of how far it might be from the truth.


## Distributions

A **probability distribution** is a *description of what a random process tends to produce*: the values that can come out, with a number attached to each saying how often.

Suppose a radio-tagged animal is located once a day, and on each day it is recorded in exactly one of five habitat patches, or as elsewhere. Each of these six possibilities is an **outcome**: on any given day exactly one of them is true, and no two can be true at once. The six together are the **sample space**, the complete list of what can happen. A distribution attaches a number between 0 and 1 to each outcome, and because the animal is somewhere every day, those six numbers sum to exactly 1.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code  code-fold="true"}
habitat <- tibble(
  patch = factor(
    c("Woodland", "Hedgerow", "Pasture", "Wetland", "Scrub", "Other"),
    levels = c("Woodland", "Hedgerow", "Pasture", "Wetland", "Scrub", "Other")
  ),
  probability = c(0.40, 0.25, 0.15, 0.1, 0.05, 0.05)
)

ggplot(habitat, aes(x = patch, y = probability)) +
  geom_col(fill = "skyblue", width = 0.7) +
  scale_x_discrete(limits = rev)+
  scale_y_continuous(limits = c(0, 0.5)) +
  labs(x = NULL, y = "Probability") +
  theme_minimal(base_size = 12)+
  coord_flip()
```

