### The normal distribution

Continuous measurements, such as body mass or wing length, are not counted out into patches; they can fall anywhere on a scale. Many of them are generated by processes in which a large number of small independent influences add together, and processes like that tend to produce the **normal distribution**: the a symmetrical bell-shaped curve, described by two numbers, the mean, where it sits, and the standard deviation, how wide it is.

Because the quantity is continuous the distribution is drawn as a smooth curve, and it is the **area** under a stretch of the curve, not the height at a point, that carries probability. The height is called a probability density, which is why the vertical axis says "density".


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code  code-fold="true"}
normal_curve <- tibble(x = seq(-4, 4, length.out = 1000)) |>
  mutate(density = dnorm(x))

band_region <- function(lo, hi, band, piece) {
  tibble(x = seq(lo, hi, length.out = 400)) |>
    mutate(density = dnorm(x), band = band, piece = piece)
}

bands <- bind_rows(
  band_region(-1, 1, "Within 1 SD", 1),
  band_region(-2, -1, "1 to 2 SD", 2),
  band_region(1, 2, "1 to 2 SD", 3),
  band_region(-3, -2, "2 to 3 SD", 4),
  band_region(2, 3, "2 to 3 SD", 5)
) |>
  mutate(band = factor(band, levels = c("Within 1 SD", "1 to 2 SD", "2 to 3 SD")))

ggplot(mapping = aes(x, density)) +
  geom_area(
    data = bands,
    aes(alpha = band, group = piece),
    fill = house_col
  ) +
  geom_line(data = normal_curve, linewidth = 0.8) +
  scale_alpha_manual(values = c(0.65, 0.40, 0.20)) +
  scale_x_continuous(
    breaks = -3:3,
    labels = c("-3", "-2", "-1", "mean", "+1", "+2", "+3")
  ) +
  labs(x = "Standard deviations from the mean", y = "Probability density", alpha = NULL) +
  theme_minimal(base_size = 12)
```

