::: {.callout-note}
## The 68-95-99.7 rule

For any normal distribution, whatever its mean and standard deviation:

- about **68%** of values fall within **1** standard deviation of the mean,
- about **95%** fall within **2**,
- about **99.7%** fall within **3**.

:::

## The distribution of an average {#sec-average}

Everything so far has described the distribution of a single thing: which patch the animal is in, how heavy one beetle is. Now apply the idea to a *summary* of a sample, which is where @sec-frequentist and the definition of a random variable come together.

Weigh five beetles and record their mean mass. Weigh five fresh beetles and record theirs. Do that many times and the means pile up into a distribution of their own, the **sampling distribution**:

:::{.callout-note}

- the **data distribution** is the spread of the individual measurements;
- the **sampling distribution** is the spread of the *statistic* across repeated samples.

:::

Confusing the two is the single most common error in everything that follows. @fig-sampling-preview shows both, for a right-skewed beetle population.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code  code-fold="true"}
beetles <- beetle_population()

mean_of <- function(n, reps) {
  map_dbl(seq_len(reps), \(i) mean(sample(beetles$mass_mg, n)))
}

preview <- withr::with_seed(demo_seed, {
  bind_rows(
    tibble(value = sample(beetles$mass_mg, 10000, replace = TRUE), kind = "Single beetles"),
    tibble(value = mean_of(5, 10000), kind = "Means of 5"),
    tibble(value = mean_of(25, 10000), kind = "Means of 25")
  )
}) |>
  mutate(kind = factor(kind, levels = c("Single beetles", "Means of 5", "Means of 25")))

ggplot(preview, aes(x = value)) +
  geom_histogram(bins = 60, fill = house_col) +
  geom_vline(xintercept = mean(beetles$mass_mg), linetype = "dashed", linewidth = 0.4) +
  facet_wrap(~kind, ncol = 1, scales = "free_y") +
  labs(x = "Body mass (mg)", y = "Count") +
  theme_minimal(base_size = 12)
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![One right-skewed beetle population, viewed three ways. Top: 10,000 individual beetles. Middle: 10,000 means, each of 5 beetles. Bottom: 10,000 means, each of 25. The dashed line is the population mean and sits in the same place in all three panels. The horizontal axis is shared, so the narrowing is real and not an artefact of rescaling. Note the free vertical scales.](01-probability_files/figure-html/fig-sampling-preview-1.png){#fig-sampling-preview width=672}
:::
:::


Three things are happening:

1. **The means are centred in the same place as the raw measurements.** Averaging does not move the target. The dashed line does not shift between panels.

2. **The means are narrower than the raw measurements, and they get narrower still as the samples get larger.** One beetle can be unusually heavy; the average of five is tamer; the average of twenty-five is tamer again. This is the same steadying you saw in the coin runs, where the running proportion was wild for a few flips and calm after a few hundred.

3. **The shape of the means tends towards the normal bell**, and this happens even though the beetles themselves are visibly skewed. The top panel has a long right tail. The bottom panel is very nearly symmetric.

Notice what is deliberately missing: a *number* for how much narrower the means get. There is a short formula for it, and deriving it is the spine of Session 2, so handing it over now would spoil the exercise. Arrive with the three facts above and the room will supply the arithmetic.

